{"causes":["The late Middle Ages saw a resurgence of interest in Franciscan spirituality, which Jacopone da Todi was deeply involved in.","The Catholic Church played a significant role in shaping the cultural and theological landscape of the time.","The rise of vernacular languages in Europe created new opportunities for literary expression.","The influence of the Byzantine Empire on Italian art and architecture also contributed to the work's development.","The availability of paper and printing technology enabled mass production and dissemination of Stabat Mater."],"effects":["Stabat Mater has had a profound impact on Christian devotion and spirituality, influencing the development of art, literature, and music.","The work's depiction of Mary's suffering and death has been a source of inspiration for countless artists, including Michelangelo and Caravaggio.","Stabat Mater has been translated into numerous languages and has undergone numerous adaptations and interpretations.","The text's use of imagery and symbolism has been studied by scholars and artists for centuries.","The work's influence can be seen in the development of various musical compositions, including the famous 'Stabat Mater' by Giuseppe Verdi.","The cultural significance of Stabat Mater extends beyond its literary merit, reflecting the cultural and theological currents of the late Middle Ages.","The work's influence on Western civilization is a testament to its enduring power and relevance.","Stabat Mater has been recognized as a masterpiece of medieval literature, with its themes and imagery continuing to resonate with audiences today."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jacopone-da-todi","sourceName":"Jacopone da Todi","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Written between 1264 and 1275."},{"sourceSlug":"franciscan-spirituality","sourceName":"Franciscan Spirituality","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Influenced by the rise of Franciscan spirituality in the late Middle Ages."},{"sourceSlug":"byzantine-empire","sourceName":"Byzantine Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Influenced Italian art and architecture during the time of Stabat Mater's creation."},{"sourceSlug":"vernacular-languages","sourceName":"Vernacular Languages","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Enabled mass production and dissemination of Stabat Mater."},{"sourceSlug":"catholic-church","sourceName":"Catholic Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"stabat-mater","targetName":"Stabat Mater","context":"Played a significant role in shaping the cultural and theological landscape of the time."}],"places":[{"name":"Umbria, Italy","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Publication Place"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Translation Location"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Stabat Mater","type":"Reference article","slug":"stabat-mater"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q210080","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stabat_Mater"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Stabat Mater contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Stabat Mater (Latin: 'The Mother Was Standing') is a medieval Catholic hymn attributed to the Italian Franciscan poet Jacopone da Todi (c. 1230–1306 CE), composed c. 1290–1300 CE and one of the most celebrated and widely set texts in the history of Western sacred music. The hymn consists of 20 stanzas in rhymed Latin verse describing the grief of the Virgin Mary at the foot of the cross during the crucifixion of Jesus — presenting a meditation on compassion (compassio), suffering, and the devotion of the believer who seeks to share in Mary's grief and in Christ's passion. The hymn begins: 'Stabat mater dolorosa / Iuxta crucem lacrimosa / Dum pendebat filius' ('The grieving mother was standing / Weeping beside the cross / While her Son was hanging there').

The Stabat Mater was widely used in Catholic liturgy and devotion — it was adopted as a sequence for the Feast of the Seven Sorrows of Our Lady and for the Stations of the Cross. Its text was set to music by virtually every major European composer from the 14th century to the 20th: Giovanni da Palestrina (c. 1590), Agostino Steffani, Alessandro Scarlatti, Vivaldi (1712), Pergolesi (1736 — the most performed setting, composed in the last weeks of his life), Haydn (1767), Boccherini, Schubert (1816), Rossini (1832/1841), Dvořák (1877), Verdi (1898), and Poulenc (1950/1951), among many others — making it the most multiply-composed Latin text other than the Mass and the Magnificat.

The Stabat Mater is a supreme expression of medieval Franciscan affective piety — the devotional tradition, pioneered by Francis of Assisi and his followers, that emphasised emotional identification with the suffering Christ and the grieving Mary as the primary method of spiritual growth. Its influence extended beyond Catholic devotion to Protestant musical culture (J.S. Bach's Widerstehe doch der Sünde is a parody of Pergolesi's Stabat Mater) and to the broader tradition of Western sacred art.","subjects":["Latin Literature","Medieval Era","Catholic Liturgy","Sacred Music","Franciscan Spirituality","Marian Devotion","Church Music","Italian Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Stabat Mater has had a profound impact on Christian devotion and spirituality, reflecting the cultural and theological currents of the late Middle Ages.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
